The current position has no immediate winning move for either player (verified by exhaustive enumeration of the 7 possible drops for \(o\) and for \(x\)). Consequently the first priority is to deny \(x\) its only one-move method of creating a threat while simultaneously advancing \(o\)'s own most flexible attacking lines.

- The unique square that lets \(x\) create a threat is column 3: the drop \((1,3)\) produces three consecutive \(x\)s on the vertical and leaves the immediately playable square \((0,3)\) as a winning threat.  
- At the same time the empty square \((2,2)\) is the intersection of several critical lines:  
  – it is the middle of the only open three-in-a-row that \(x\) can still form on row 2;  
  – it is the key connecting square of \(o\)'s most accessible diagonal \((4,0)\)-\((3,1)\)-\((2,2)\)-\((1,3)\);  
  – it begins a vertical pair for \(o\) in column 2 that can be turned into a threat with one further drop.  

Occupying column 2 therefore accomplishes three things at once:  
1. it permanently prevents \(x\) from ever obtaining the horizontal three on row 2 (and the two latent winning eyes at \((2,0)\) and \((2,4)\) that would accompany it);  
2. it advances \(o\)'s diagonal and vertical attacking options;  
3. it forces \(x\) either to answer on column 3 (creating a single threat that \(o\) can simply block) or to surrender the initiative, after which \(o\) can itself create a threat on the following move.

No other column simultaneously neutralises \(x\)'s only immediate threat-creation resource and improves \(o\)'s own counter-play. After the forced exchange on column 3, \(o\) still retains a clean two-move method of manufacturing a new threat (the vertical in column 2) while \(x\)'s remaining attack (the diagonal eye at \((2,4)\)) requires two preparatory drops and can be answered in time. Thus column 2 is the unique move that keeps the position balanced and preserves \(o\)'s winning chances.

Final Answer: 2

